#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""known-limits-check: every limit in a PR body's Known-limits block carries a follow-up or a permanent acceptance.

usage: known-limits-check [--body-file FILE ...] [--tracker-cmd CMD]

A PR description that accepts a known limit on the condition that a follow-up ticket be filed loses the
limit when nobody files it: nothing fails until a reviewer notices, rounds later. This check makes the
escape loud when the body is written or reviewed. The reviewer brief (reviewer-brief.md section 1) runs it
on the body as filed. Design: find loosely, judge strictly, fail loudly on anything in between.

The body is prepared first. Fenced code blocks are dropped; from HTML comments only the comment text is
removed, so the rest of the line keeps its meaning, and a fence or comment that never closes is an error,
because what follows it would silently vanish from the scan. The trailer — the LAST "Decisions:" or
"**Decisions:**" line and everything after it — is dropped, but only when no Known-limits block or stray
follows that line: a "Decisions:" label in the summary with a block after it is body text, not a trailer,
and cutting it would drop the block. Then every line is classified, from its text
alone: blank, heading, rule ("---"), list item, continuation (2+ spaces or a tab), label (a bold-led line,
or "Word: ..." at column 0 with up to four words before the colon and no "//"), or paragraph.

A Known-limits block opens at one of three forms, matched without case over the label words "known limits",
"known limitations", "limitations", "accepted limits" and "accepted limitations":
  A. a heading whose text is a label word, with an optional parenthetical suffix and/or colon
     ("## Known limits", "## Known limits (v1):"); a heading that mixes the label with other words
     ("## Known limitations in this PR") is an error, not silence.
  B. a label word alone on its line, optionally bold-wrapped and/or with a colon
     ("**Known limits**", "Known limits:").
  C. a label word followed by a colon and text, on its own line at column 0
     ("Known limits: X. Follow-up: PROJ-1.", "**Known limits:** None") — a one-unit block over that
     line and its lazy continuation lines.
A list item that starts with a label word ("- Known limits: X") is never a block: it is a stray, loud
whether or not it carries a ticket, because the check does not read that form.

A heading block ends before the next heading of the same or higher level, a rule, or another Known-limits
start; deeper headings and label-only lines inside it are group titles, kept for context and not judged —
so a list under a group title inside a heading block is read as limits. A bold or "Label:" block ends
before the next heading, rule, another start, or ANY label line — a bold group title ("**Export**"), a
plain label-only line ("Also:", "Testing:"), or a bold run-in line ("**Emoji:** prints ?"); that any
label line ends such a block is accepted (decision #1259, widening #1258). Group limits under a
"## Known limits" heading block instead, where label lines are group titles. An inline block ends at the
first blank line or the next label line. Every block found prints one span line: its lines, its unit
count, and what ended it, so a boundary is visible on every run, pass or fail.

Outside blocks, a line is a stray when a heading, bold-led line, list item or "> " quote starts with a
label word, or a paragraph starts with "known limit" / "accepted limit" after leading ">", quote and
emphasis markers ("*Known limits*", "> **Known limits**") are stripped, or a paragraph starts with bare
"Limitations" alone or followed by a colon. Backticks are not stripped: a line that opens with a
backticked label is code, not a mention. An empty block fails.

Judging is strict. Units, not physical lines: every list item at any depth is a unit, and so is every
paragraph, except a lead-in paragraph ending in ":" that is directly followed by a list. Nesting: a list
item nests under the open item indented less than it, and an item at or above the block's first item's
indent is top-level, so a list indented by one to three spaces is still flat. Continuation: an item
includes its indented continuation lines and a lazy continuation (a non-blank line directly after it, with
no blank line between). Coverage counts only on the unit that carries it, with no inheritance: every leaf
needs its own marker, and a parent with children passes without one only when its text ends in ":" (a
group label). The markers are "Follow-up: KEY", "Tracked: KEY", "Tracked in KEY", a "/browse/KEY" link, a
GitHub issue URL (https://.../issues/N), "accepted as permanent (decision #N)", or the alias
"Permanent: decision N". The marker words match without case; the key does not, so "follow-up: x86-64" is
not coverage. A bare key with no marker fails, with the one-word fix. A block whose only content is "None"
or "n/a" passes with zero limits; "None" next to listed limits is an error.

The body text comes from --body-file files (repeatable) or stdin when no file is given. Exit 0 every block
passes (a body with no Known-limits block passes); 1 an error, a stray line, an empty block, or an
uncovered limit; 2 bad arguments, an unreadable file, or the tracker command could not be run. All
failures are printed, and every failing run ends with a short how-to footer.

With --tracker-cmd CMD (or KNOWN_LIMITS_TRACKER_CMD in the environment, when the flag is absent) every
reference that carries a marker is confirmed: CMD runs once per distinct reference with it as the only
argument — the key for KEY-shaped and /browse/ references, the full URL for a GitHub issue link — and
exit 0 means the ticket or issue exists and is open. Without a tracker command the check stays on shape
only, so it works wherever the dotfiles deploy, with or without a tracker.
"""
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys

LABEL_SRC = r"known\s+limitations|known\s+limits|accepted\s+limitations|accepted\s+limits|limitations"
LABEL_RE = re.compile(LABEL_SRC, re.IGNORECASE)
HEADING = re.compile(r"^(#{1,6})\s+(.*)$")
LIST_ITEM = re.compile(r"^(\s*)(?:[-*+]|\d+[.)])\s+(.*)$")
RULE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:-\s*){3,}$|^\s*(?:\*\s*){3,}$|^\s*(?:_\s*){3,}$")
BOLD_SPAN = re.compile(r"^\*\*[^*]{1,60}\*\*|^__[^_]{1,60}__")
BOLD_ONLY = re.compile(r"^\*\*[^*]{1,60}\*\*:?\s*$|^__[^_]{1,60}__:?\s*$")
LABEL_KIND = re.compile(r"^[A-Z][^:\n]{0,39}:(\s|$)")
LABEL_ONLY = re.compile(r"^[A-Z][^:\n]{0,39}:\s*$")
KEY = re.compile(r"\b[A-Z][A-Z0-9]+-[0-9]+\b")  # case-sensitive: a key is uppercase
# the marker words match without case; the key does not (x86-64 is not a ticket)
MARKER = re.compile(r"\b(?i:follow-up|tracked)\s*(?::|(?i:in)\b)\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9]+-[0-9]+)")
BROWSE = re.compile(r"/browse/([A-Z][A-Z0-9]+-[0-9]+)")
ISSUE = re.compile(r"https?://\S+/issues/\d+\b")
PERMANENT = re.compile(r"accepted\s+as\s+permanent\s*\(\s*decision\s*#\s*\d+\s*\)", re.IGNORECASE)
PERMANENT_ALIAS = re.compile(r"permanent\s*:\s*(?:decision\s*#?|#)\s*\d+", re.IGNORECASE)
NONE_TEXT = re.compile(r"^(?:none|n/a)\.?$", re.IGNORECASE)
TRUNC = 60


def trunc(text):
    return text if len(text) <= TRUNC else text[:TRUNC - 3] + "..."


def classify(text):
    """One line's kind, from its text alone: blank, heading, rule, item, continuation, label, paragraph."""
    if not text.strip():
        return "blank"
    if text[0] == "#" and HEADING.match(text):
        return "heading"
    if RULE.match(text):
        return "rule"
    if LIST_ITEM.match(text):
        return "item"
    if text.startswith("\t") or text.startswith("  "):
        return "continuation"
    if BOLD_SPAN.match(text) or (LABEL_KIND.match(text) and "//" not in text.split(":")[0]):
        return "label"
    return "paragraph"


def form_a(htext):
    """True when a heading's text is a label word, with an optional parenthetical suffix and/or colon."""
    t = htext.strip()
    if t.endswith(":"):
        t = t[:-1].strip()
    if LABEL_RE.fullmatch(t):
        return True
    return bool(re.match(r"^(" + LABEL_SRC + r")\s*\([^()]*\)$", t, re.IGNORECASE))


def form_b(stripped):
    """True when the whole line is a bold-wrapped or plain label word, optionally with a colon."""
    t = stripped.strip()
    m = re.match(r"^\*\*(.*)\*\*\s*:?\s*$", t) or re.match(r"^__(.*)__\s*:?\s*$", t)
    if m:
        inner = m.group(1).strip()
        if inner.endswith(":"):
            inner = inner[:-1].strip()
    else:
        inner = t[:-1].strip() if t.endswith(":") else t
    return bool(LABEL_RE.fullmatch(inner))


def form_c(stripped):
    """The text after the label when the line is 'Known limits: X' or '**Known limits:** X' at column 0;
    None when it is not that form."""
    t = stripped.strip()
    m = re.match(r"^(" + LABEL_SRC + r")\s*:\s*(.*)$", t, re.IGNORECASE)
    if m:
        return m.group(2).strip()
    m = re.match(r"^\*\*(.+?)\*\*\s*:?\s*(.*)$", t)
    if m:
        inner = m.group(1).strip()
        rest = m.group(2).strip()
        had_colon = inner.endswith(":") or rest.startswith(":")
        if inner.endswith(":"):
            inner = inner[:-1].strip()
        if had_colon and LABEL_RE.fullmatch(inner):
            return rest.lstrip(":").strip()
    return None


def start_form_at(lines, i):
    """None, "A", "B" or "C" — does the line at index i open a Known-limits block?"""
    text = lines[i][1]
    kind = classify(text)
    stripped = text.strip()
    if kind == "heading":
        return "A" if form_a(HEADING.match(stripped).group(2)) else None
    if kind == "label":
        if form_b(stripped):
            return "B"
        if form_c(stripped):
            return "C"
    return None


def stray_mention(kind, text):
    """True when a line outside every block is a label mention this check does not read (A1).
    Backticks are not stripped: a line that opens with a backticked Known limits is code, not a mention."""
    t = text.strip()
    if kind == "heading":
        return bool(LABEL_RE.match(HEADING.match(t).group(2)))
    if kind == "item":
        return bool(LABEL_RE.match(LIST_ITEM.match(text).group(2).strip()))
    if kind == "label":
        return bool(LABEL_RE.match(re.sub(r"^(\*\*|__)", "", t)))
    if kind == "paragraph":
        t2 = re.sub(r"^>\s*", "", t)
        t2 = re.sub(r"^[\*_]+", "", t2)  # italics, bold runs and quotes before the label word (f5)
        m = LABEL_RE.match(t2)
        if not m:
            return False
        if m.group(0).lower().replace(" ", "").startswith(("knownlimit", "acceptedlimit")):
            return True
        rest = t2[m.end():].strip()
        return rest == "" or rest.startswith(":")
    return False


def prepare(text):
    """Drop fenced code blocks, HTML comments and the Decisions trailer; keep original line numbers.
    Returns (lines, errors): an unclosed fence or comment is an error, because what follows it would
    silently vanish from the scan."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    kept = []
    errors = []
    in_fence = False
    fence_char = ""
    fence_len = 0
    fence_line = 0
    in_comment = False
    comment_line = 0
    for no, line in enumerate(lines, 1):
        if in_fence:
            s = line.strip()
            if s.startswith(fence_char * fence_len) and set(s) <= {fence_char}:
                in_fence = False
            continue
        if in_comment:
            # inside a multi-line comment: keep only what follows the closing -->
            if "-->" in line:
                in_comment = False
                kept.append((no, line.split("-->", 1)[1]))
            else:
                kept.append((no, ""))
            continue
        m = re.match(r"^\s*(`{3,}|~{3,})", line)
        if m:
            in_fence = True
            fence_char = m.group(1)[0]
            fence_len = len(m.group(1))
            fence_line = no
            continue
        if "<!--" in line:
            # remove only the comment text; the rest of the line stays (f1)
            reduced = re.sub(r"<!--.*?-->", "", line)
            if "<!--" in reduced:
                in_comment = True
                comment_line = no
                reduced = reduced.split("<!--", 1)[0]
            kept.append((no, reduced))
            continue
        kept.append((no, line))
    if in_fence:
        errors.append((fence_line, "a code fence opens here and never closes, so the rest of the body "
                                   "cannot be read. Close it."))
    if in_comment:
        errors.append((comment_line, "an HTML comment opens here and never closes, so the rest of the body "
                                     "cannot be read. Close it with -->."))
    # the trailer is the LAST Decisions line and everything after it — unless a Known-limits start or a
    # stray follows that line, in which case it is a label in the body, not a trailer, and cutting would
    # drop the block that follows it (f2)
    cut = None
    for idx, (no, line) in enumerate(kept):
        s = line.strip()
        if s.startswith("Decisions:") or s.startswith("**Decisions:**"):
            cut = idx
    if cut is not None:
        for k in range(cut + 1, len(kept)):
            text = kept[k][1]
            blocked = start_form_at(kept, k) is not None
            if not blocked:
                kind = classify(text)
                blocked = kind in ("heading", "item", "label", "paragraph") and stray_mention(kind, text)
            if blocked:
                cut = None
                break
    if cut is not None:
        kept = kept[:cut]
    return kept, errors


def end_of_block(lines, i, form, level):
    """(end index exclusive, reason) for a form A or B block that opens at index i."""
    j = i + 1
    n = len(lines)
    while j < n:
        text = lines[j][1]
        stripped = text.strip()
        if start_form_at(lines, j) is not None:
            return j, "another Known limits start"
        kind = classify(text)
        if kind == "heading":
            lvl = len(HEADING.match(stripped).group(1))
            if form == "B" or lvl <= level:
                return j, 'heading "%s"' % trunc(stripped)
            j += 1
            continue
        if kind == "rule":
            return j, 'rule "%s"' % trunc(stripped)
        if form == "B" and kind == "label" and (BOLD_SPAN.match(stripped) or LABEL_ONLY.match(stripped)):
            return j, 'label "%s"' % trunc(stripped)
        j += 1
    return n, "end of body"


def find_blocks(lines, errors):
    """Classify lines and find Known-limits blocks (design sections 5 to 7, amended A1 to A3, A5).
    Returns the blocks; appends (line, message) errors for strays and extra-words headings."""
    blocks = []
    i = 0
    n = len(lines)
    while i < n:
        no, text = lines[i]
        stripped = text.strip()
        form = start_form_at(lines, i)
        if form is None:
            kind = classify(text)
            if kind == "heading":
                htext = HEADING.match(stripped).group(2)
                if re.search(r"known limit|accepted limit", htext, re.IGNORECASE):
                    errors.append((no, 'heading "%s" mixes known limits with other words. Use a heading '
                                   'of its own, "## Known limits", and move other content to its own '
                                   'heading.' % trunc(stripped)))
                    i += 1
                    continue
            if kind in ("heading", "item", "label", "paragraph") and stray_mention(kind, text):
                errors.append((no, '"%s" is written in a form this check does not read. Use "## Known '
                                   'limits" or "**Known limits**" on its own line, followed by a list.'
                               % trunc(stripped)))
            i += 1
            continue
        if form == "A":
            level = len(HEADING.match(stripped).group(1))
            end, reason = end_of_block(lines, i, "A", level)
            group_titles = set()
            for k in range(i + 1, end):
                s = lines[k][1].strip()
                if classify(lines[k][1]) == "label" and (BOLD_ONLY.match(s) or LABEL_ONLY.match(s)):
                    group_titles.add(lines[k][0])
            blocks.append({"form": "A", "opener": no, "content_start": i + 1, "end": end,
                           "reason": reason, "group_titles": group_titles})
            i = end
            continue
        if form == "B":
            end, reason = end_of_block(lines, i, "B", None)
            blocks.append({"form": "B", "opener": no, "content_start": i + 1, "end": end, "reason": reason})
            i = end
            continue
        # form C: a one-unit inline block; the opener line is its content
        j = i + 1
        reason = "end of body"
        while j < n:
            t2 = lines[j][1]
            if not t2.strip():
                reason = "blank line"
                break
            if start_form_at(lines, j) is not None:
                reason = "another Known limits start"
                break
            kind2 = classify(t2)
            if kind2 == "heading":
                reason = 'heading "%s"' % trunc(t2.strip())
                break
            if kind2 == "rule":
                reason = 'rule "%s"' % trunc(t2.strip())
                break
            if kind2 == "label":
                # the next section's label ends the inline block even with no blank between (f3)
                reason = 'label "%s"' % trunc(t2.strip())
                break
            j += 1
        blocks.append({"form": "C", "opener": no, "content_start": i, "end": j, "reason": reason})
        i = j
    return blocks


def parse_units(block_lines, group_titles=None):
    """Build the unit tree for a block's lines. Returns (units, has_list).
    group_titles is a set of line numbers to treat as group labels (kept for context, not units)."""
    units = []
    stack = []  # open list items as (indent, unit)
    para = None
    prev_blank = True
    top_indent = None
    state = {"has_list": False}

    def flush_para():
        nonlocal para
        if para is not None:
            units.append(para)
            para = None

    for no, text in block_lines:
        stripped = text.strip()
        if not stripped:
            prev_blank = True
            continue
        if HEADING.match(stripped) or (group_titles and no in group_titles):
            # a sub-heading, or a label-only line in a heading block, is a group label, not a unit;
            # it does not end the block
            flush_para()
            prev_blank = False
            continue
        indent = len(text) - len(text.lstrip())
        m = LIST_ITEM.match(text)
        if m:
            state["has_list"] = True
            flush_para()
            item_indent = len(m.group(1))
            unit = {"kind": "item", "first": no, "last": no, "parts": [m.group(2).strip()], "children": []}
            if top_indent is None:
                top_indent = item_indent
            if item_indent <= top_indent:
                stack.clear()
                units.append(unit)
            else:
                while stack and stack[-1][0] >= item_indent:
                    stack.pop()
                if stack:
                    stack[-1][1]["children"].append(unit)
                else:
                    units.append(unit)
            stack.append((item_indent, unit))
            prev_blank = False
            continue
        if para is not None and not prev_blank:
            para["parts"].append(stripped)
            para["last"] = no
            prev_blank = False
            continue
        if stack and (indent > stack[-1][0] or not prev_blank):
            # continuation of the innermost open item: indented, or lazy (no blank between)
            stack[-1][1]["parts"].append(stripped)
            stack[-1][1]["last"] = no
            prev_blank = False
            continue
        flush_para()
        para = {"kind": "para", "first": no, "last": no, "parts": [stripped], "children": []}
        prev_blank = False
    flush_para()

    # a lead-in paragraph ending in ":" directly followed by a list is covered by that list, not a unit
    kept = []
    for u in units:
        if u["kind"] == "para" and u["parts"][-1].endswith(":"):
            k = 0
            for idx, (no, t) in enumerate(block_lines):
                if no == u["last"]:
                    k = idx + 1
                    break
            while k < len(block_lines) and not block_lines[k][1].strip():
                k += 1
            if k < len(block_lines) and LIST_ITEM.match(block_lines[k][1]):
                continue
        kept.append(u)
    return kept, state["has_list"]


def marker_keys(text):
    """References that carry a marker: Follow-up: KEY, Tracked: KEY / Tracked in KEY, a /browse/KEY link,
    or a GitHub issue URL. Keys come back as keys; issue URLs come back as the full URL."""
    return MARKER.findall(text) + BROWSE.findall(text) + ISSUE.findall(text)


def bare_keys(text, keys):
    """KEY-shaped tokens in text that no marker covers."""
    marked = set(keys)
    return [k for k in KEY.findall(text) if k not in marked]


def judge_unit(u, name, failures, marked_keys, counts):
    """Judge one unit on its own text; children are judged independently (no inheritance)."""
    for c in u["children"]:
        judge_unit(c, name, failures, marked_keys, counts)
    text = " ".join(u["parts"])
    keys = marker_keys(text)
    if keys or PERMANENT.search(text) or PERMANENT_ALIAS.search(text):
        marked_keys.update(keys)
        counts[0] += 1
        return
    if u["children"] and text.rstrip().endswith(":"):
        return  # a group label: its children carry the limits
    bare = bare_keys(text, keys)
    if bare:
        failures.append((u["first"], '"%s" is not marked as coverage. Bare keys are not read, because "UTF-8" looks like one. '
                         'Add "Follow-up: %s" before it.' % (bare[0], bare[0])))
        return
    hint = " If this starts a new section, give it a heading or a label." if u["kind"] == "para" else ""
    failures.append((u["first"], 'limit has no follow-up: "%s". Add "Follow-up: PROJ-123" or '
                                 '"accepted as permanent (decision #N)" to this item.%s' % (trunc(text), hint)))


def none_text_of(t):
    """A unit's text with a leading (possibly bold-wrapped) label and colon removed, for the None check."""
    t = t.strip()
    t = re.sub(r"^(?:\*\*\s*)?(?:" + LABEL_SRC + r")\s*:?\s*(?:\*\*)?\s*", "", t, flags=re.IGNORECASE)
    return t.strip()


def check_body(text, name):
    """Print one span line per block and one line per failure; return (failures, marked keys, limits, blocks)."""
    lines, errors = prepare(text)
    blocks = find_blocks(lines, errors)
    failures = list(errors)
    marked_keys = set()
    counts = [0]
    for idx, b in enumerate(blocks, 1):
        block_lines = lines[b["content_start"]:b["end"]]
        units, has_list = parse_units(block_lines, b.get("group_titles"))
        flat = []

        def collect(us):
            for u in us:
                flat.append(u)
                collect(u["children"])

        collect(units)
        nonblank = [no for no, t in block_lines if t.strip()]
        start_line = nonblank[0] if nonblank else b["opener"]
        end_line = nonblank[-1] if nonblank else b["opener"]
        if b["end"] < len(lines):
            print("known-limits: block %d, lines %d-%d, %d unit(s), ended by %s at line %d"
                  % (idx, start_line, end_line, len(flat), b["reason"], lines[b["end"]][0]))
        else:
            print("known-limits: block %d, lines %d-%d, %d unit(s), ended by %s"
                  % (idx, start_line, end_line, len(flat), b["reason"]))
        # "None" / "n/a" as the only content passes with zero limits; together with items it is an error
        texts = [" ".join(u["parts"]) for u in flat]
        none_idx = [k for k, t in enumerate(texts) if NONE_TEXT.match(none_text_of(t))]
        if none_idx:
            if len(texts) > len(none_idx):
                failures.append((flat[none_idx[0]]["first"], '"None" appears in a Known limits block that '
                               'also lists limits. Remove "None" or the items.'))
            continue
        if not flat:
            failures.append((b["opener"], "the Known limits block has no list items. List each limit as "
                                          '"- ...", or write "Known limits: None". If the next line is a '
                                          'bold or "Label:" line, it ended the block: group limits with '
                                          '"###" sub-headings or nested lists, and drop intro lines.'))
            continue
        for u in units:
            judge_unit(u, name, failures, marked_keys, counts)
    for no, msg in failures:
        print("known-limits-check: %s: line %d: %s" % (name, no, msg), file=sys.stderr)
    return len(failures), marked_keys, counts[0], len(blocks)


def confirm(tickets, tracker):
    """Run the tracker command once per key; return the count of tickets it does not confirm."""
    bad = 0
    for key in sorted(tickets):
        try:
            done = subprocess.run(tracker + [key], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
            print("known-limits-check: the tracker command failed on %s: %s" % (key, e), file=sys.stderr)
            raise
        if done.returncode != 0:
            bad += 1
            print("known-limits-check: ticket %s is not confirmed: the tracker command exited %d%s"
                  % (key, done.returncode, (": " + done.stderr.strip()) if done.stderr.strip() else ""), file=sys.stderr)
            print("  the ticket must exist and be open, or the limit needs a different follow-up", file=sys.stderr)
    return bad


def usage(why):
    print("known-limits-check: %s" % why, file=sys.stderr)
    print("usage: known-limits-check [--body-file FILE ...] [--tracker-cmd CMD]", file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(2)


def main(argv):
    bodies = []
    tracker = None
    args = list(argv)
    while args:
        a = args.pop(0)
        if a == "--body-file":
            if not args:
                usage("--body-file needs a file")
            bodies.append(args.pop(0))
        elif a.startswith("--body-file="):
            bodies.append(a.split("=", 1)[1])
        elif a == "--tracker-cmd":
            if not args:
                usage("--tracker-cmd needs a command")
            tracker = shlex.split(args.pop(0))
        elif a.startswith("--tracker-cmd="):
            tracker = shlex.split(a.split("=", 1)[1])
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        else:
            usage("unknown argument %r" % a)
    if tracker is None:
        env = os.environ.get("KNOWN_LIMITS_TRACKER_CMD", "").strip()
        if env:
            tracker = shlex.split(env)
    if not bodies:
        bodies = ["-"]

    texts = []
    for body in bodies:
        try:
            if body == "-":
                texts.append((body, sys.stdin.read()))
            else:
                with open(body, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                    texts.append((body, fh.read()))
        except OSError as e:
            print("known-limits-check: cannot read %s: %s" % (body, e), file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    bad = 0
    marked_keys = set()
    limits = 0
    blocks = 0
    for name, text in texts:
        body_bad, body_keys, body_limits, body_blocks = check_body(text, name)
        bad += body_bad
        marked_keys.update(body_keys)
        limits += body_limits
        blocks += body_blocks

    if tracker is not None and marked_keys:
        try:
            bad += confirm(marked_keys, tracker)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            return 2

    if bad:
        print("known-limits-check: FAIL: %d problem(s) in the Known-limits block(s)" % bad, file=sys.stderr)
        print("How to write known limits:", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  ## Known limits", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  - <limit> (Follow-up: PROJ-123)", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  - <limit>: accepted as permanent (decision #4)", file=sys.stderr)
        print('  The block ends at the next heading, "---", or "Label:" / **Label** line.', file=sys.stderr)
        print("  No limits:  Known limits: None", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    suffix = ", tracker confirmed" if tracker is not None and marked_keys else ""
    print("known-limits-check: OK (%d limit(s) in %d block(s), all covered%s)" % (limits, blocks, suffix))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
